# Every push to main triggers a new Netlify build with newly-hashed JS/CSS
# filenames (Vite's default). Without an explicit rule here, Netlify's
# default caching behaviour for plain .html files is not guaranteed strict
# enough to stop a browser from holding onto an OLD index.html/3d.html across
# a redeploy — and this project redeploys constantly (dissertation sync is a
# standing rule, and this session alone pushed six times in under two
# hours). A stale HTML page references a build's own specific hashed asset
# filenames; if the browser doesn't refetch the HTML after a new deploy, it
# can end up requesting assets from a build state that no longer matches
# what's live, which reads as exactly what was reported live 2026-09-09:
# the /3d viewer working once right after a fresh deploy, then intermittently
# hanging on a later visit with no code change in between. Force every HTML
# entry point to always revalidate, while the hashed, content-addressed
# assets underneath /assets/ are safe to cache forever (a new build always
# gets new filenames, never reuses old ones).
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
